From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 00:11:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Sweep the manuscript simulation across SNR

smi_manuscript_60deg.m ran at a single SNR = 50 and 25 realisations. It now
runs NREP = 1000 realisations across SNR_LIST = [5 10 20 30 50 Inf].

Layout. The sweep is one contiguous block of voxels per SNR, each block
holding every condition at NREP realisations in the voxel order
gen_montecarlo.m uses. Step 6 hands one block at a time to SMI.fit, so no fit
ever sees two noise levels at once - the same decomposition the pipeline uses,
one invocation per SNR. Each block is seeded from mc_config.m's seed offset by
the SNR index, so a noise level is reproducible on its own and adding or
removing an entry does not change the realisations of the others.

Inf is carried as a genuine infinity rather than as the pipeline's 1e4 stand-in.
sigma = 0 passes through SMI.fit unharmed - the Rician correction subtracts
zero and the noise draw reduces to the signal exactly - so the only cost is
that the checks dividing by sigma are special-cased instead of printing NaN.
Two properties follow and are now checked: that block must recover the true
fibre count wherever the truth itself resolves, and being noiseless it must
come back bit identical, so its standard deviation is exactly 0.

Scoring. Step 7 reports correct fibre count, mean angular error, its standard
deviation and spurious peaks per voxel, for every Lmax and SNR. The ceiling is
no longer scored by a second copy of the peak finder: the band-limited truth is
prepended as the first row of every scored block, so it goes through the same
lines as the realisations, and the Inf row and the ceiling row agree by
construction.

The peak finder is rewritten against precomputed neighbour sets. The
max(a .* nbr, [], 1) form built a full 1500x1500 array per voxel and at 12000
voxels would have dominated the runtime of the file. The two are equivalent,
not merely similar: with nbr logical every non-neighbour contributes exactly 0
to that maximum, so the old threshold is max(0, neighbourhood max), and it is
only ever compared against amplitudes that have already passed a > 0.

Figures. Figure 3 is transposed - b runs down the rows, SNR across the columns,
increasing left to right and ending at the ground truth, which makes the Inf
column and the truth column two different computations of the same object.
Figure 4 is split per condition into Figures 4 and 5, each with the Figure 1
ground truth glyph as its first column, truncated to the row's Lmax so it is
the same object Step 7 scores the ceiling on. Figure 6 is new: bias, spread
and spurious peak count against SNR, drawn from the arrays Step 7 prints so the
plots and the tables cannot disagree.

Step 5 also prints which of SMI's noise-level bins each SNR will land in.
SMI does not fit the kernel at the sigma passed in - it bins the normalised
sigma into Nlevels bins over sigma_norm_limits and trains one regression per
occupied bin, evaluated at the bin centre - so two SNRs can share a regression
and anything below SNR 5 is clamped to a bin trained at a lower noise level
than the data has. Only a sweep makes that visible.

The export now covers the whole sweep in one image per Lmax, and
voxel_key.txt carries an SNR column.

Runtime is hours rather than minutes at these defaults: 18 fits, and at
NREP = 1000 the per-voxel deconvolution is no longer negligible next to the
regression training. NREP is the knob and the file says so.

Verified statically only, as requested - Octave parse check, a
bracket-depth-aware keyword balance check, a use-before-assignment scan, and a
harness that runs the Step 5 and Step 7 blocks extracted verbatim against
synthetic fODFs. No simulation was run.

